</span></div>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Improve Classification Ability of VLMMs</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our preliminary experiments, we observed that VLMMs struggle to capture the semantic shift introduced by new updates. To address this, we integrate counterfactual reasoning to explore the diverse logical and semantic relationships among the update, premise, and hypothesis, thereby mitigating inherent inference biases. Specifically, we propose a Chain of Counterfactual Thought Classification framework that encapsulates three key modules—<span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Counterfactual Thought-induced Rationale Generation</span>, <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ASR-enhanced Video Content Integration</span>, and <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rationale Selection and Refinement</span>—as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.2 ASR-Enhanced Video Content Integration ‣ 5 Improve Classification Ability of VLMMs ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Counterfactual Thought-Induced Rational Generation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To capture the complexity of logical semantic relationships among the update, premise, and hypothesis, we leverage VLMMs to generate two rationales for each sample—one explaining why the update acts as a Strengthener and the other explaining why it serves as a Weakener. This approach enables counterfactual reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weinzierl and Harabagiu, 2024</a>)</cite> by examining both perspectives for each case as follows,</p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{w},R_{s}=\operatorname{VLMM}(P(H,U),V)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>VLMM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{w},R_{s}=\operatorname{VLMM}(P(H,U),V),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(H,U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(H,U)</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt as shown in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Prompts in our Classification Framework ‣ Appendix B Prompts ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>. <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the rationales generated by the model, where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> explains why the update weakens the hypothesis, and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> explains why the update strengthens it.
Our method prompts the model to analyze the update from two opposing perspectives: (1) assuming it strengthens the hypothesis and (2) assuming it weakens the hypothesis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>ASR-Enhanced Video Content Integration</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After generating the two rationales, we must determine which one is more plausible as the final answer. However, because vision-based fine-tuning can sometimes impair an LLM’s original generative or reasoning capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we rely on a separate LLM to evaluate which rationale is stronger. Since this LLM cannot directly process visual information, its selection process may introduce biases or yield suboptimal results. To address this, we convert the video content into a textual description, enabling the LLM to incorporate the visual context when comparing the two rationales. Specifically, we input the video into VLMMs to generate the textual description,
formulated as
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}=\operatorname{VLMM}(V,P_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>VLMM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}=\operatorname{VLMM}(V,P_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the generated description. <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt, as shown in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Prompts in our Classification Framework ‣ Appendix B Prompts ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, existing VLMMs often generate incomplete or inaccurate video descriptions, as they primarily rely on visual inputs and may overlook crucial auditory information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nishimura et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
This issue is especially problematic in Defeasible Video Entailment task since the spoken dialogue conveys key semantic details.
To address this, we refine video descriptions by integrating VLMM-generated description with ASR transcripts, ensuring a more comprehensive and context-aware representation of the video.
Specifically, we first extract spoken dialogue from the video via an ASR engine to capture verbal context, and then employ an LLM to integrate the ASR transcript with the VLMM-generated description as follows,</p>
<table id="S5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{A}=\operatorname{ASR}(A)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ASR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{A}=\operatorname{ASR}(A),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D=\operatorname{LLM}(P_{A}(T_{A},D^{\prime}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>LLM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D=\operatorname{LLM}(P_{A}(T_{A},D^{\prime})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the refined description and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the audio. <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ASR text. <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{A}(T_{A},D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{A}(T_{A},D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt, as shown in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Prompts in our Classification Framework ‣ Appendix B Prompts ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.
This refinement is particularly important in scenarios where key details—such as causal relationships between events or character motivations—are conveyed primarily through dialogue rather than visual cues.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22385v1/ICCV25-method.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/257;" width="476" height="257" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The architecture of Chain of Counterfactual Thought Classification Framework, including three modules: Counterfactual Thought-Induced Rational Generation, ASR-Enhanced Video Content Integration, and Rationale Refinement and Selection.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22385v1/ICCV25-method-gen.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/101;" width="476" height="101" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The architecture of LLM-Guided ASR-Integrated Generation Framework, including ASR-Enhanced Video Content Integration, and LLM-Refined Update Generation.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Rationale Refinement and Selection</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we mentioned before, because vision-based fine-tuning can sometimes impair an LLM’s original generative or reasoning abilities, we rely on a separate LLM to evaluate which rationale is stronger. In addition, we occasionally observe that the rationales for both weakener and strengthener are not reasonable. Therefore, we also ask the LLM to refine the rationale by revising reasoning errors before it outputs the final answer,
formulated as
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\operatorname{LLM}(P_{F}(R_{s},R_{w},D,H,U))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>LLM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\operatorname{LLM}(P_{F}(R_{s},R_{w},D,H,U))</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{F}(R_{s},R_{w},D,H,U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{F}(R_{s},R_{w},D,H,U)</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt detailed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Prompts in our Classification Framework ‣ Appendix B Prompts ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>. <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is the final result, including the refined rationals and the selection result.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Improve Generation Ability of VLMMs</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we mentioned before, there are two limitations in VLMMs: 1) missing key audio information and 2) cannot fully capture the entailment semantics between update, hypothesis, and premise.
To address the limitations, we proposed an LLM-Guided ASR-Integrated Generation Framework using the modules devised in our classification framework, as shown in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Problem Formulation ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Specifically, we first refine VLMM-generated descriptions by ASR-Enhanced Video Content Integration, ensuring more accurate textual representations of the video content.
This step is identical to the refinement process in the classification task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the classification framework, we use the LLM to refine VLMM-generated updates based on these refined descriptions, premise, hypothesis, and goal to further refine the update as
<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\operatorname{LLM}(P_{U}(D,H,P,G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>LLM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\operatorname{LLM}(P_{U}(D,H,P,G))</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{U}(D,H,P,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{U}(D,H,P,G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt, as shown in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Prompts in our Generation Framework ‣ Appendix B Prompts ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>. <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the refined description. <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the generated update.
By leveraging LLM, our method can revise the reasoning errors in VLMMs and capture the real entailment semantics. The overall architecture of the model is illustrated in
Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 ASR-Enhanced Video Content Integration ‣ 5 Improve Classification Ability of VLMMs ‣ Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters—or Double-Down:A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
